---
import ReportOpening from '../components/ReportOpening.astro';
import ProductionLayout from '../layouts/ProductionLayout.astro';
---
<ProductionLayout title="Roadmap — Skyhook Industries" description="Open questions for the orbital tether and Dyson swarm, the measurements each decision needs and the proposed steps toward hardware.">
  <ReportOpening>
    <p class="eyebrow">Engineering program</p>
    <h1>What would let us build it?</h1>
    <p>The immediate work is to put numbers on the decisions that could stop a skyhook or a solar collector from working. These are questions a contributor can investigate today, followed by the proposed path to hardware.</p>
    <Fragment slot="figure">
      <img class="specimen" src="/studies/tether-specimen.webp" width="1280" height="680" alt="Three braided load paths joined by fine diagonal links and held between two bolted test grips" />
      <figcaption><b>Multiline tether in tensile grips</b><span>Proposed materials test</span></figcaption>
    </Fragment>
  </ReportOpening>

  <section class="paper-section" id="open-questions" aria-labelledby="open-heading">
    <div class="section-grid">
      <p class="chapter-label">Open research questions</p>
      <div>
        <h2 id="open-heading">Evidence that could change the design</h2>
        <div class="gate-list questions">
          <article class="gate" id="tether-strength">
            <span>Tether structure</span>
            <div><h3>What strength survives the joint?</h3><p>For the 15 t payload case, the ideal mass budget needs 5.86 MJ/kg after derating. Report a terminated line’s failure load and linear density from the same specimen, with the sample count, service conditions and safety allowance. If the usable value falls short, rerun the mass budget with that value.</p></div>
            <aside><a href="/reference-architecture/#mass-closure">Reproduce the mass threshold →</a></aside>
          </article>
          <article class="gate" id="capture-load">
            <span>Payload capture</span>
            <div><h3>Can the grapple stop the relative motion within its travel?</h3><p>Start with a speed mismatch and the stroke available to absorb it. Compare the calculated load with a measured force–displacement curve. A peak load above the attachment limit would change the grapple design or the allowable rendezvous error.</p></div>
            <aside><a href="/lab/capture/">Run the capture calculation →</a></aside>
          </article>
          <article class="gate" id="collector-heat">
            <span>Dyson swarm</span>
            <div><h3>How much usable power can a hot panel produce?</h3><p>Size one collector at two solar distances. Replace its assumed conversion efficiency and optical properties with values measured at its operating temperature. See whether moving inward still reduces the deployed area for the same electrical output.</p></div>
            <aside><a href="/research/dyson-swarm/">Compare collector cases →</a></aside>
          </article>
        </div>
        <a class="cta" href="/contact/#engineering">Contribute evidence or a correction →</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="dark-section">
    <div class="section-grid">
      <p class="chapter-label">Program logic</p>
      <div>
        <h2>From a reference design to an orbital test</h2>
        <p class="body-copy">Each step earns the next. Close the calculations, test the materials, then demonstrate the dynamics and payload handoff at increasing scale. Progress depends on measured results and the exit conditions below.</p>
        <div class="gate-list">
          <div class="gate"><span>GATE 01</span><div><h3>Close the reference architecture.</h3><p>Reference Architecture v0.4 now closes one 4,000 km symmetric screening geometry, ideal tapered mass versus payload/material performance, instantaneous post-capture COM change, and the first symmetric-versus-asymmetric balance trade. Full post-capture propagation, release targeting and real material-system mass remain open.</p></div><aside>STATUS / first geometry + ideal mass closure complete; dynamics still open</aside></div>
          <div class="gate"><span>GATE 02</span><div><h3>Prove the material system.</h3><p>Move beyond headline tensile strength. Characterize allowable specific strength after joints, manufacturing defects, creep, fatigue, thermal cycling, atomic oxygen, radiation and safety factor.</p></div><aside>EXIT / coupon + joint data support structural model assumptions</aside></div>
          <div class="gate"><span>GATE 03</span><div><h3>Prove dynamics at small scale.</h3><p>Build and operate a rotating tether test article that exercises deployment, control, load redistribution, sensor fusion, sectional behavior and representative failure cases.</p></div><aside>EXIT / measured dynamics match the control model across normal + faulted cases</aside></div>
          <div class="gate"><span>GATE 04</span><div><h3>Fly an orbital tether demonstrator.</h3><p>Deploy a modest tether in orbit and verify deployment, long-duration dynamics, inspection, attitude/orbit control and survivability assumptions in the actual environment.</p></div><aside>EXIT / stable controlled tether operation over a meaningful mission duration</aside></div>
          <div class="gate"><span>GATE 05</span><div><h3>Demonstrate momentum exchange.</h3><p>Use a controlled payload handoff to measure capture loads, facility-state change and release accuracy. The experiment can be small; the physics and control loop must be real.</p></div><aside>EXIT / capture → swing → release demonstrated with closed energy/momentum accounting</aside></div>
          <div class="gate"><span>GATE 06</span><div><h3>Recover and reuse.</h3><p>Restore the tether’s orbital/rotational state after a momentum-exchange event and repeat the operation. Electrodynamic reboost is the leading study path, but the gate is about repeatability rather than allegiance to one mechanism.</p></div><aside>EXIT / repeat mission after measured state recovery</aside></div>
          <div class="gate"><span>GATE 07</span><div><h3>Move the rendezvous downward.</h3><p>Progressively raise closing speed and lower the handoff altitude using increasingly flight-like vehicles, navigation and capture hardware.</p></div><aside>EXIT / high-altitude vehicle capture with credible operational margins</aside></div>
          <div class="gate"><span>GATE 08</span><div><h3>Build transportation infrastructure.</h3><p>Only after the preceding gates are closed does a multi-thousand-kilometre operational tether become a procurement and deployment problem instead of a collection of assumptions.</p></div><aside>EXIT / system-level case closes technically, operationally and economically</aside></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="paper-band">
    <section class="paper-section">
      <div class="section-grid">
        <p class="chapter-label">What scales first</p>
        <div>
          <h2>Scale knowledge before hardware.</h2>
          <p class="lede">The first useful prototypes do not need to resemble the final tether. Their job is to make the models wrong in informative ways.</p>
          <div class="fact-table">
            <div class="fact-row"><b>Structure</b><p>Break specimens, joints and redundant sections before trying to build heroic lengths.</p><small>Early</small></div>
            <div class="fact-row"><b>Controls</b><p>Use instrumented rotating rigs and deployment tests to validate estimation and control laws.</p><small>Early</small></div>
            <div class="fact-row"><b>Capture</b><p>Test moving-target acquisition, retention and load transfer independently before orbital integration.</p><small>Early</small></div>
            <div class="fact-row"><b>Orbit</b><p>Use increasingly realistic simulation and then small orbital hardware to expose environmental and operational coupling.</p><small>Progressive</small></div>
          </div>
          <a class="cta light" href="/reference-architecture/">Read the current reference architecture →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="paper-section">
      <div class="section-grid">
        <p class="chapter-label">Decision rule</p>
        <div>
          <h2>If a gate fails, change the machine.</h2>
          <p class="lede">The ~4,000 km study length, symmetric geometry, material family, capture architecture and reboost method are not sacred. A failed gate should force a design change, not a rhetorical defense of the old baseline.</p>
          <a class="cta light" href="/research/">See the research the program builds from →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
  <section class="dark-section" id="finlay-study">
    <div class="section-grid">
      <p class="chapter-label">Mission study / 15P Finlay</p>
      <div>
        <h2>Bring a water source into the network.</h2>
        <p class="lede">Alongside the tether program, the Finlay study asks whether a comet could supply its own redirection propellant and become an orbital water depot. These study gates define what must be learned before choosing a mission architecture.</p>
        <div class="gate-list">
          <div class="gate"><span>STUDY 01</span><div><h3>Characterize the target.</h3><p>Constrain Finlay's nucleus size, mass, spin, activity and accessible volatiles. Develop a reconnaissance and rendezvous plan from orbital observations.</p></div><aside>EXIT / a target model with explicit uncertainty and a credible survey path</aside></div>
          <div class="gate"><span>STUDY 02</span><div><h3>Contain and extract.</h3><p>Study deployment around an irregular rotating nucleus, distributed attachment loads, dust handling, volatile collection and heat rejection. Start with small representative samples and containment tests.</p></div><aside>EXIT / measured extraction, leakage and load limits support the design</aside></div>
          <div class="gate"><span>STUDY 03</span><div><h3>Close the propulsion budget.</h3><p>Account for the water spent as propellant, thermal power, thrust duration and changing mass. Compare plane-change strategies within the full trajectory rather than assuming inclination alone sets the maneuver.</p></div><aside>EXIT / a coupled mass, power and trajectory budget preserves a useful water reserve</aside></div>
          <div class="gate"><span>STUDY 04</span><div><h3>Choose and reach a depot orbit.</h3><p>Compare destinations around Earth, the Moon and Mars. Solve approach, capture, long-term stability and control, including fragmentation and loss-of-thrust cases.</p></div><aside>EXIT / propagated transfers and depot operations meet explicit constraints</aside></div>
        </div>
        <p class="body-copy">The current workbench screens propulsion and plane-change assumptions. Full mission trajectories, whole-nucleus containment and depot capture remain open work.</p>
        <a class="cta light" href="/missions/finlay/">Inspect the Finlay mission study →</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</ProductionLayout>
